  "duration": 50.04,
  "text": "Your heart beats about 100,000 times every day, pumping nearly 5 liters of blood every minute. But how does this fist-sized muscle actually work? The heart is divided into four chambers. The two on top are called the right atrium and left atrium. The two on the bottom are the right ventricle and left ventricle. Blood returning from your body arrives empty of oxygen. It enters the right atrium, then drops into the right ventricle, which pumps it straight to the lungs. In the lungs, the blood picks up fresh oxygen. It travels back to the left atrium, then falls into the left ventricle, the strongest chamber of the heart. With a single powerful squeeze, the left ventricle launches oxygen-rich blood through the aorta, sending it to every cell in your body. This entire round trip takes less than one second, and your heart will repeat it roughly 2.5 billion times in your lifetime.",
